00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
so you're calling for the logistics  industry to quote get its house in order  yes indeed we're still about 70 000 htv  drivers short we seem to have these  periodic challenges where we can't get  goods or fuel  to those who really need it and then the  government has to step in but government  can only put sticking plasters on there  so what we'd really like to see is the  industry given  a set of measures that they have to  carry out deliver more service stations  pay for the training of hgv drivers uh  that needs to be accompanied by reforms  in the planning process if the industry  then don't deliver those then that's  where i believe that the government  should step in deliver it and then put  the  tax back on to industry and i'm not  talking about the small haulers here i'm  talking about the parts of the supply  chain either at the beginning that tends  to be the big oil companies say or the  the end the sale part that tends to be  the online giants the retailers those  are where the profits are made and i  didn't think that's where we need to  look at the charging mechanism rather  than them just saying look it's nothing  to do with us um you know we just  contract i think the industry just needs  to get his entire house in order and i  think his levy would really focus  attention because we keep making these  recommendations that things need to be  done but they never are so i think by  putting a levy over the top of the  industry they'll really have the clock  ticking to deliver  what do you what do you think it says  about the industry that has taken a  global pandemic for these conditions to  come to light  well it's disappointing i think it's  really disappointing if you look at the  workforce as a whole so only one percent  of the workforce are female  and only three percent are under 25 and  that really just demonstrates that  younger people don't want to go into  this market anymore um for those that  are older it's been a way of life to  sleep above the cab but younger people  don't want to do that  the markets change they can take sort of  shorter routes  jobs so driving for  online deliveries um and women won't  work in the workforce because they don't  have the facilities they have to share  with the men well who's going to do that  um you know people need to be treated  with a lot more respect it's not just  about pay i should say it's about making  sure the facilities are there the  respects there the overnight stays are  safe and people don't feel threatened as  they do from crime  um it's just a systematic sea change  that's needed and i think the industry  as a whole  owns the supply of goods and then the  sale they need to own  the way that that supply occurs and the  shortcomings within it  okay um i want to talk to you now about  what's happening at our airport what is  happening at our airports queues  cancellations people are missing their  weddings their holidays are what's going  on  well what we've got is a perfect storm  where the market has really bounced back  faster perhaps than people that  anticipated  and the aviation sector just can't get  the staff in place fast enough  both because  staff are taking other jobs there are  1.2 million vacancies  and also links to this is because it can  take three months to security vet and  train the staff and of course some of  those staff just don't want to wait that  long they want to be earning so they're  not taking the jobs  i think it's disappointing for the  government to  appear to blame the industry this is an  industry that's lost billions they can't  just flick the switch on in anticipation  because there have been so many uh  changes to the rules  they've had to wait until there's  clarity and equally to say well actually  they've ramped up too much demand  parliament and government have told  industry if they don't  use 70 of the  flight slots then they'll lose them so  we've effectively told them to fly so i  think we need to find the solutions  between government and industry  to help the industry cope and make sure  that people's holiday experiences  are not spoiled it's not all airlines  it's just some virgin haven't cancelled  a single flight but uh clearly it's a  miserable experience for far too many do  you think they're going to be able to  sort everything out  by the summer holidays because it's  going to get worse than  before it even gets better  well no that will be a challenge i mean  one of the airports told me that they  are  needing 400  sort of ground  staff that have to be properly security  vetted  only 100 of those have been vetted it  takes such a long time i think that's  where we can see uh government and  industry work together so perhaps whilst  some of these security betting is going  on at the end of it um then the  classroom training can take place  perhaps we can also look at the numbers  of trainees and  the supervisors at the moment you have  to have one supervisor for three  trainees perhaps we can make it more to  the line of sight so i think there is  more that government can do with  industry but that's not going to happen  if they're both at each other's throats  as uh appears to be from this morning  okay mr merriman i want to talk to you  about the prime minister now in february  you said that he needed to ship up or  shape out and do you think it is now  time for him to step aside  no i don't and i think it's linked to  some of the issues we've just been  talking about the policy ideas that i'd  like to see put into practice which i  believe will help people and you can't  do that if you change the prime minister  because people just assume that it's  just one switch one change a bit like a  football manager being changed and then  a new person comes in runs the same team  plays the same match it doesn't work  like that with government everything  stops everyone changes policy gets held  up and we're we're at a really difficult  time for people right now not just on  their ability to take their holiday  or get goods transported as i've been  discussing but the cost of living crisis  that matters hugely to people and for  the sake of i think the most vulnerable  people that we represent we need to  focus on delivering policy change for  them and if you deliver regime change  then you can't get the policies through  that people rely on us for so my feeling  is that um he's wrong has occurred he's  apologized put his hands up for that i  judge people for what they do to turn  things around and i feel that he needs  to be given that time to do so  so you're definitely not putting a  letter in are you  i have definitely not as a lettering  and i will not be putting a letter in  because if i do that i'm then  responsible for  the very policy recommendations that  i've been making through my committee  not being delivered uh and so as far as  i'm concerned he yes has to be held to  the highest uh standards yes things have  gone wrong that shouldn't have gone  wrong but now i'll judge him on the  basis of what he does in terms of  delivering for the people that need us  the most you say people are worried  about the cost of living crisis do you  not think they're also worried about the  fact that his own ethics advisor is said  to be considering his position  over the pm's attitude to the  ministerial code and does that not worry  you  yes i i don't want to make light of the  the situation at all it does concern me  and i've spoken before about how  disappointing it is and the people that  set the rules uh follow the rules that's  the most important thing uh and we have  to ensure the ministerial code is  followed as well i've seen the exchanges  between the prime minister and  the his advisor  and yes of course it concerns me but  what concerns me even more than that  is finding fixes solutions to the  challenges that are causing people to  live  really challenging existences right now  and i just don't feel that we can  provide those solutions if we see the  prime minister change so i think it's  important the prime minister reflects on  what's occurred he said that he'll do so  and he'll do better we then judge him to  an even higher standard on that basis  but i'd like him to be given the time to  make those changes  so far 41 conservative mps have  expressed doubt in the pm's permission  position excuse me do you think there  will be a confidence vote next week  well i honestly don't know that there's  only one man that does and that's the  man that collects the letters and he  wouldn't be telling me so i don't know  how many people have put letters in i  don't know if there will therefore be be  a challenge but my point more perhaps my  appeals to colleagues  is that you know our constituents need  us right now and they need the  government to deliver and parliament to  deliver we're not going to be able to do  that if we are going through a  protracted leadership contest it just  changes all the focus all the direction  uh all of the chances we have of making  better changes in policy that will help  people  through difficult times  you can't have both so i think people  either focus on the theater  and want to change direction and i  respect their position if they do want  that but you can't then expect  government to be able to get on and  govern and deliver policy which is  ultimately the most important thing for  me so everyone will have their own views  to make i respect those that feel we've  had enough i just feel like we need to  change

